    number: 1,
    content: 'Which paragraph mentions the potential of blockchain to reduce transaction costs in international trade?',
    passage: '[A] Blockchain technology, originally developed as the underlying framework for Bitcoin, has evolved far beyond its cryptocurrency origins. At its core, a blockchain is a distributed ledger that records transactions across a network of computers. Each block contains a cryptographic hash of the previous block, a timestamp, and transaction data, making it virtually impossible to alter past records without detection.\n\n[B] One of the most promising applications of blockchain lies in supply chain management. By providing a transparent, immutable record of every transaction and movement of goods, blockchain can help companies track products from origin to destination. This is particularly valuable in industries like pharmaceuticals, where counterfeit drugs pose a significant threat to public health. Walmart, for example, has implemented blockchain to trace the origin of its food products, reducing the time needed to identify contamination sources from seven days to just seconds.\n\n[C] In the financial sector, blockchain has the potential to dramatically reduce transaction costs in international trade. Traditional cross-border payments involve multiple intermediaries—correspondent banks, clearing houses, and payment processors—each adding fees and processing time. Blockchain-based systems can streamline these processes by enabling direct peer-to-peer transactions, potentially saving billions of dollars annually. Ripple, a blockchain-based payment protocol, has already partnered with over 300 financial institutions worldwide.\n\n[D] However, blockchain technology faces significant scalability challenges. The Bitcoin network, for instance, can process only about seven transactions per second, compared to Visa\'s capacity of over 50,000 transactions per second. Various solutions have been proposed, including sharding (splitting the blockchain into smaller pieces) and layer-2 protocols like the Lightning Network, but none have yet achieved the scalability needed for mainstream adoption.\n\n[E] Energy consumption is another major concern. Bitcoin mining alone consumes more electricity than some medium-sized countries. The proof-of-work consensus mechanism that secures Bitcoin requires miners to solve increasingly complex mathematical puzzles, consuming enormous amounts of computational power. Some newer blockchains have adopted proof-of-stake mechanisms, which are significantly more energy-efficient, but the transition poses technical and governance challenges.\n\n[F] Regulatory uncertainty also clouds the future of blockchain. Different countries have adopted vastly different approaches, from China\'s ban on cryptocurrency trading to El Salvador\'s adoption of Bitcoin as legal tender. The European Union has introduced the Markets in Crypto-Assets (MiCA) regulation, attempting to create a comprehensive framework. In the United States, the regulatory landscape remains fragmented, with different agencies claiming jurisdiction over different aspects of blockchain technology.\n\n[G] Despite these challenges, enterprise adoption of blockchain continues to grow. According to a 2024 survey by Deloitte, 39% of organizations worldwide are using blockchain in some capacity, up from 23% the previous year. The most common applications include supply chain tracking, digital identity verification, and smart contracts—self-executing agreements that automatically enforce their terms when predefined conditions are met.\n\n[H] The concept of decentralized finance (DeFi) represents another frontier. DeFi platforms aim to recreate traditional financial services—lending, borrowing, trading, insurance—without intermediaries, using smart contracts on blockchain networks. While DeFi has attracted billions in investment, it has also been plagued by hacks, fraud, and extreme volatility, raising questions about its long-term viability.\n\n[I] Looking ahead, the convergence of blockchain with other emerging technologies—artificial intelligence, the Internet of Things, and 5G networks—could unlock entirely new applications. Imagine supply chains where IoT sensors automatically record data on a blockchain, AI algorithms analyze the data to optimize logistics, and smart contracts automatically execute payments upon delivery. This vision of fully automated, transparent commerce may still be years away, but the foundational pieces are being laid today.',
