    passage: 'The gig economy has fundamentally altered the landscape of modern employment. Platforms like Uber, DoorDash, and TaskRabbit have created millions of opportunities for workers seeking flexibility and autonomy. Proponents argue that gig work empowers individuals to set their own schedules, diversify their income streams, and achieve a better work-life balance. However, critics point to a darker reality. Many gig workers lack basic protections such as health insurance, paid leave, and retirement benefits. The classification of these workers as independent contractors rather than employees has become a contentious legal issue in many jurisdictions. In 2023, the European Commission proposed legislation that would presume gig workers to be employees if their platform meets certain criteria related to control and supervision. Meanwhile, research from the Massachusetts Institute of Technology suggests that when accounting for expenses such as vehicle maintenance and fuel, many gig workers earn below minimum wage. The debate raises fundamental questions about the social contract between workers, employers, and governments in an era of increasing labor market flexibility.',
