    content: 'What is the main idea of the passage about cognitive biases?',
    passage: 'Cognitive biases—systematic patterns of deviation from rationality in judgment—have profound implications for decision-making in every domain of human life. First catalogued by psychologists Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman in the 1970s, these mental shortcuts, or heuristics, evolved to help our ancestors make quick decisions in dangerous environments. However, in the complex modern world, they often lead to predictable errors. Confirmation bias, for example, causes people to seek out information that confirms their existing beliefs while ignoring contradictory evidence. This phenomenon has been amplified by social media algorithms that create "echo chambers," reinforcing users\' preconceptions. The anchoring effect leads people to rely too heavily on the first piece of information they encounter when making decisions. In salary negotiations, for instance, the initial number proposed often sets the range for the entire discussion, regardless of its objective merit. Understanding these biases is not merely an academic exercise. Organizations that implement "debiasing" strategies—such as structured decision-making processes, diverse teams, and pre-mortem analyses—have been shown to make better strategic decisions. Some governments have even established "nudge units" that apply behavioral insights to design policies that account for human cognitive limitations.',
